WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.157
This pattern finds the latest admission for each patient.

00:00:03.157 --> 00:00:07.962
For the current patient, the inner query finds the maximum admission date.

00:00:07.962 --> 00:00:13.032
The outer admission row is kept when its date equals that patient-specific maximum.

00:00:13.032 --> 00:00:20.162
This pattern is useful for latest order, latest event, newest status, or most recent activity.

00:00:20.162 --> 00:00:24.712
If two rows share the same maximum date, both can be returned.

00:00:24.712 --> 00:00:30.197
Connect this idea to a compliance workflow checking whether related evidence exists.

00:00:30.197 --> 00:00:35.392
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:35.392 --> 00:00:42.382
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:42.382 --> 00:00:47.822
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:47.822 --> 00:00:52.960
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
